  "worked_solution": "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)}{6}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\pi )}{6}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
